    "dailyNotes": "### Lecture & Lab Synthesis — Upper GI Histology (Dr. Vinida Bundit)\n- **4-Layer Diagnostic Framework (Mandatory for Practical & IMS Exams)**:\n  1. **Mucosa**: Epithelium + Lamina propria (loose CT, capillaries, lymphatics, MALT) + Muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle; produces local mucosal movement/folding).\n  2. **Submucosa**: Dense irregular connective tissue + **Meissner's (submucosal) nerve plexus** (parasympathetic secretomotor control) + glands ONLY in **Esophagus** and **Duodenum**.\n  3. **Muscularis Externa**: Inner circular layer + **Auerbach's (myenteric) nerve plexus** (peristaltic motility) + Outer longitudinal layer (stomach adds an innermost oblique layer).\n  4. **Adventitia vs. Serosa**: Adventitia blends directly into surrounding fascia (thoracic esophagus); Serosa is covered by a glistening simple squamous **mesothelium** (visceral peritoneum).\n- **Barrett's Esophagus Correlation**: Chronic gastroesophageal reflux (GERD) damages the non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the lower esophagus, inducing **incomplete intestinal metaplasia** into **simple columnar epithelium with Goblet cells** — the primary precursor to esophageal adenocarcinoma.",
