      "Definition of a Hernia (Handwritten Note): A visceral organ protruding from its original anatomical space through a congenital or acquired wall defect.",
      "Omphalocele vs. Gastroschisis: Omphalocele = midline defect THROUGH the umbilical ring, herniated viscera/liver are COVERED by a 3-layer membranous sac (amnion - Wharton's jelly - peritoneum) with the umbilical cord inserting onto the apex of the sac; caused by failure of midgut loops to return from physiological herniation at week 10; strongly associated with chromosomal trisomies (13, 18, 21) and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome. Gastroschisis = paraumbilical defect (almost always to the RIGHT of a normal umbilicus), NO covering sac, bowel floats freely in amniotic fluid (thickened matted inflammatory peel/serositis); usually an isolated defect in young mothers.",
      "Urachus & Umbilical Ligaments (Handwritten Note): The urachus is the embryonic remnant of the allantois connecting the dome of the fetal urinary bladder to the umbilicus; it normally obliterates into the Median Umbilical Ligament. Failure to obliterate causes a Patent Urachus (clear urine draining from the umbilicus in a neonate), Urachal Cyst, or Urachal Sinus. (Medial umbilical ligaments = obliterated umbilical arteries; Lateral umbilical folds = inferior epigastric vessels).",
      "Indirect vs. Direct Inguinal Hernia: Both protrude ABOVE the inguinal ligament. Indirect Inguinal Hernia originates LATERAL to the inferior epigastric vessels, enters the deep inguinal ring via a patent processus vaginalis, traverses the inguinal canal alongside the spermatic cord, and exits the superficial inguinal ring into the scrotum (covered by all 3 spermatic fascial layers). Direct Inguinal Hernia protrudes MEDIAL to the inferior epigastric vessels directly through a weakened transversalis fascia within Hesselbach's Triangle (bounded by Inferior epigastric vessels laterally, Rectus abdominis medially, and Inguinal ligament inferiorly).",
      "Femoral Hernia: Protrudes BELOW the inguinal ligament and LATERAL to the pubic tubercle through the femoral ring into the femoral canal (medial to the femoral vein inside the femoral sheath). More common in females; carries the HIGHEST risk of incarceration and strangulation due to the rigid borders of the femoral ring (especially the sharp medial lacunar ligament)."
