    "deepUnderstanding": "Your handwritten notes in `Untitled Notebook(1).pdf` highlight three exam-critical distinctions for abdominal wall defects: (1) **Congenital Umbilical vs. Paraumbilical Defects**: During week 6 of gestation, the midgut rapidly elongates and physiologically herniates into the umbilical cord, rotating 90 degrees counterclockwise around the SMA axis before returning to the abdominal cavity at week 10 (completing a total 270-degree counterclockwise rotation). If the midgut fails to return at week 10, an **Omphalocele** results (central, covered by a peritoneal-amniotic sac, umbilical cord inserts on the sac). Conversely, full-thickness ischemic weakness of the right paraumbilical body wall (involution of the right umbilical vein) produces **Gastroschisis** (right of intact umbilicus, uncovered bowel exposed to irritating amniotic fluid).\n(2) **Embryonic Duct Remnants at the Umbilicus**: A baby with discharge from the umbilicus has two classic differentials: **Patent Urachus** (persistent allantois connecting bladder to umbilicus -> drains **urine**, becomes the median umbilical ligament) vs. **Vitelline (Omphalomesenteric) Fistula / Meckel's Diverticulum** (persistent vitelline duct connecting ileum to umbilicus -> drains **feculent/bilious intestinal content**).\n(3) **Groin Hernia Landmarks**: Use two anatomical lines: the **Inguinal Ligament** (ASIS to pubic tubercle) separates inguinal hernias (above/superomedial to pubic tubercle) from **femoral hernias** (below/inferolateral to pubic tubercle, medial to femoral vein). Inside the groin, the **Inferior Epigastric Vessels** separate **Indirect** inguinal hernias (lateral, congenital patent processus vaginalis) from **Direct** inguinal hernias (medial, acquired weakness in Hesselbach's triangle).",
