    "deepUnderstanding": "Pages 27–30 of `Source_Files/Histo .pdf` contain the official 3-station Lower GI Histology Lab Quiz with your circled answers:\n- **Trap #1 (Villi vs. Microvilli Cores — Quiz 1B)**: The **lamina propria** (loose connective tissue with capillaries, smooth muscle fibers, plasma cells, and a central lacteal) forms the core of a **villus**, whereas a **microvillus** is a 1-micron apical cytoplasmic projection supported by a bundle of **actin filaments** anchored in the terminal web.\n- **Trap #2 (Muscularis Mucosae vs. Muscularis Externa — Quiz 2A/2B)**: The thin smooth muscle layer immediately beneath the intestinal crypts is the **muscularis mucosae** (functions for local **mucosal motility** to expel crypt secretions and enhance absorption), whereas the thick smooth muscle coat outside the submucosa is the **inner circular layer of the muscularis externa**.\n- **Trap #3 (Where Paneth Cells Are Absent — Quiz 3A/3B)**: At the **anorectal junction**, the simple columnar epithelium with straight tubular crypts full of goblet cells abruptly transitions to stratified squamous epithelium. Normal large intestine and rectum crypts **do NOT contain Paneth cells** (finding Paneth cells in the distal colon is abnormal *Paneth cell metaplasia*, a hallmark of chronic Inflammatory Bowel Disease such as Ulcerative Colitis!).",
