    "dailyNotes": "### Complete In-Class Cases & Viral/Bacterial Master Comparison (`Source_Files/`)\n#### 1. Viral Gastroenteritis Diagnostic & Clinical Matrix (`11 1 Intestinal microbiology Virology part- in class.pdf`)\n| Virus | Genome / Capsid | Epidemiology & Peak | Key Mechanism / Clinical Feature | Shedding (/g stool) | **1st-Line Lab Test** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Rotavirus** | Naked 3-layered **dsRNA** (11 segments, Reovirus) | **#1 severe diarrhea in infants** (6–24 mo); Winter peak | Villus-tip lysis ($\\downarrow$ lactase $\\rightarrow$ **stool pH < 5.5**, **reducing substance $\\ge 2+$**) + **NSP4 enterotoxin** ($\\uparrow\\text{Ca}^{2+}$, inhibits SGLT1) | **$10^{11}$** (Massive) | **Antigen (ELISA / IC / LA)** |\n| **Norovirus** | Naked icosahedral **(+)ssRNA** (Calicivirus) | **#1 epidemic viral gastroenteritis (all ages)**; 54% foodborne (**shellfish/seafood salad**); Winter | Villous blunting, brush-border enzyme loss (no enterotoxin); **prominent vomiting**, resolves in 1–3 d | $7 \\times 10^7$ | **RT-PCR** |\n| **Astrovirus** | Naked star-shaped **(+)ssRNA** | Young children (<2 yr), daycare, elderly | Mild watery diarrhea (2–4 d), less vomiting | Moderate | **RT-PCR** |\n| **Sapovirus** | Naked 'Star of David' **(+)ssRNA** (Calicivirus) | Infants & toddlers | Milder than Norovirus | Moderate | **RT-PCR** |\n| **Enteric Adenovirus (40, 41)** | Naked icosahedral **dsDNA** | **Infants < 6 months**; year-round (no season) | **Prolonged diarrhea (7–14 days)** | High | **Antigen (ELISA/IC) or PCR** |\n\n#### 2. Rotavirus Vaccines (Oral Live-Attenuated — Start at Age 2 Months)\n- **Rotarix** (Monovalent human G1P[8]): **2 doses** at **2 and 4 months**.\n- **RotaTeq** (Pentavalent human-bovine reassortant G1, G2, G3, G4, P[8]): **3 doses** at **2, 4, and 6 months**.\n- **Rotavac** (Monovalent human G9P[11]) & **Rotasiil** (Pentavalent bovine reassortant): **3 doses** at **2, 4, and 6 months**.\n- *Historical Adverse Event Alert*: 1st-generation rhesus rotavirus vaccine (RotaShield) was withdrawn due to **intussusception**.\n\n#### 3. Bacterial Pathogenesis Pearl Summary (`GI Week 1.pdf` Handwritten Notes)\n- **EPEC**: Bundle-forming pili (BFP) + **T3SS injects Tir** into enterocyte membrane $\\rightarrow$ binds outer membrane **Intimin** $\\rightarrow$ actin polymerization **pedestal** & attaching-effacing (A/E) lesion.\n- **ETEC**: Colonization factor antigens (CFA) + **Heat-labile toxin (LT)** ($\\text{AB}_5$, ADP-ribosylates $\\text{G}_s\\alpha \\rightarrow \\uparrow\\text{cAMP}$, like cholera toxin) + **Heat-stable toxin (STa)** (activates guanylate cyclase $\\rightarrow \\uparrow\\text{cGMP}$).\n- **EAEC**: Aggregative adherence fimbriae (AAF) form a **'stacked-brick' biofilm** + mucinase + ST-like/plasmid-encoded toxins.\n- ***Shigella***: Non-motile (no flagella); invades M cells $\\rightarrow$ lyses vacuole $\\rightarrow$ **IcsA** polar actin tail propels bacteria laterally into adjacent enterocytes $\\rightarrow$ induces macrophage apoptosis (IL-1$\\beta$) and PMN transmigration (IL-8).\n- ***Campylobacter jejuni***: Curved/gull-wing Gram-negative rod; microaerophilic, thermophilic (**42°C**); poultry reservoir; causes bloody diarrhea, pseudoappendicitis, **Guillain-Barré syndrome** (anti-GM1 ganglioside molecular mimicry), and **reactive arthritis**.\n- ***Clostridioides difficile***: Antibiotic use (clindamycin, cephalosporins, fluoroquinolones) wipes out normal flora $\\rightarrow$ spore germination $\\rightarrow$ **Toxin A (enterotoxin)** & **Toxin B (cytotoxin)** **glucosylate Rho family GTPases** $\\rightarrow$ depolymerize actin filaments $\\rightarrow$ tight junction loss & **pseudomembranous colitis**.",
