    "deepUnderstanding": "In `Source_Files/LabGI_enteric bact pathogens_CUMEDi_slide.pdf`, Assoc. Prof. Kanitha Patarakul lays out the exact laboratory workflow tested on both the IMS-III written exam and the BCC-I OSCE station:\n1. **Why Stool Culture Requires Selective Media & Timed Enrichment**: Because normal colonic flora ($>10^{11}\\text{ bacteria/g}$ of anaerobes and lactose-fermenting coliforms like *E. coli*) vastly outnumber enteric pathogens, plating stool on blood agar is useless. Instead, Day 1 combines direct plating on **MacConkey**, **SS**, **MSRV**, and **TCBS** agars with liquid enrichment in **Tetrathionate broth** (strictly limited to **8–12 hours** so normal flora do not break through inhibition) and **Alkaline Peptone Water (2% NaCl, pH 8.6)**.\n2. **How to Read the 4 Plates at a Glance**:\n   - On **MacConkey** and **SS agar**, lactose fermenters (*E. coli*, *Klebsiella*) produce acid that turns the neutral red indicator pink/red, so you **ignore pink colonies** and pick **colorless colonies** (*Salmonella*, *Shigella*). On **SS agar**, sodium thiosulfate and ferric citrate reveal $\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$ production as a **black center** (*Salmonella* = colorless with black center; *Shigella* = purely colorless).\n   - On **MSRV semi-solid agar** (incubated at **42°C**), motile non-typhoidal *Salmonella* swim outward from the central inoculation drop, creating an opaque **turbid halo**.\n   - On **TCBS agar (pH 8.8)**, bromthymol blue turns **yellow** when sucrose is fermented (***Vibrio cholerae***) and remains **blue-green** when sucrose is not fermented (***Vibrio parahaemolyticus***).\n3. **Why We Don't Routinely Culture *C. difficile***: *C. difficile* is a strict anaerobe and non-toxigenic strains can colonize the gut asymptomatically. Therefore, the lab uses the **C. DIFF QUIK CHEK COMPLETE** rapid cassette EIA to detect **GDH antigen** (left line) and **Toxin A/B** (right line) around the central dotted control line within 30 minutes.",
