    "deepUnderstanding": "Session 13 connects Upper GI microbial pathogenesis to clinical endoscopy and pathology:\n1. **Why Location of *H. pylori* Colonization Determines Duodenal vs. Gastric Ulcer**: *H. pylori* thrives in the mucus layer overlying neutral mucin-secreting cells. In patients with high baseline parietal cell mass, *H. pylori* colonizes predominantly the **pyloric antrum** (which lacks acid-secreting parietal cells). Antral inflammation depletes **D cells** (somatostatin), removing the brake on **G cells** (gastrin). Elevated gastrin hyperstimulates the intact parietal cells in the gastric body, flooding the **duodenal bulb (D1)** with acid and causing **duodenal ulcers**. Conversely, when *H. pylori* colonizes the entire stomach (**pangastritis/corpus-predominant**), direct mucosal inflammation and IL-1$\\beta$/TNF-$\\alpha$ suppress and destroy parietal cells, causing **hypochlorhydria**, **gastric ulcers** (classically at the incisura angularis on the lesser curvature), **intestinal metaplasia**, and **gastric adenocarcinoma**.\n2. **Where to Biopsy in Viral Esophagitis (HSV vs. CMV Exam Classic)**: Because **HSV-1** infects squamous epithelial cells directly, you must biopsy the **edge/margin** of a punched-out vesicle/ulcer to see multinucleated giant cells and Cowdry type A inclusions. Because **CMV** infects submucosal vascular endothelial cells and fibroblasts, you must biopsy the **deep base/center** of a large linear ulcer to see 'owl's-eye' cytomegalic inclusions.",
