      "13-Step Nasogastric (NG) Intubation OSCE Checklist (Handwritten Notes in Source_Files/Nasogastric Intubation .pdf): (1) Introduce yourself & verify patient identity, (2) Explain procedure, risks, benefits & indications, (3) Obtain informed consent, (4) Check and prepare all equipment, (5) Perform hand hygiene & wear gloves, (6) Measure NEX tube length: Earlobe -> Tip of Nose -> Epigastrium/Xiphoid process, (7) Position patient sitting upright at 30-45 degrees (neck slightly flexed once in pharynx), (8) Lubricate the distal tip of the NG tube with water-soluble jelly, (9) Insert gently along the floor of the nose (perpendicular to face, aiming toward the ear) into the nasopharynx, (10) Ask the patient to SWALLOW WATER (or saliva) while advancing the tube smoothly to the measured mark, (11) Verify gastric placement via 3 methods: (a) Aspirate gastric contents, (b) Insufflate air with syringe while auscultating over the epigastrium for a gurgling 'whoosh', (c) Confirm with Chest/Abdominal X-ray (gold standard before feeding), (12) Secure tube to the nose with tape, (13) Check that the patient can SPEAK clearly (rules out endotracheal intubation).",
      "3 Major Indications for NG Intubation (Handwritten Notes): (1) Diagnostic & Therapeutic Gastric Aspiration/Lavage: detect Upper GI bleeding, aspirate blood/clots, replace/irrigate with normal saline, or collect gastric fluid for pathogen detection (e.g., swallowed sputum in pediatric/uncooperative TB), (2) Gastric Decompression: relieve gas and fluid distension in small bowel obstruction, paralytic ileus, or gastric outlet obstruction, (3) Enteral Feeding & Medication Administration: provide nutrition or drugs in patients with impaired swallowing (dysphagia/stroke) who have an intact GI tract.",
      "3 Major Contraindications to NG Intubation (Handwritten Notes): (1) Caustic / Corrosive (Strong Acid or Alkali) Ingestion: high risk of perforating a friable, necrotic esophagus or inducing reflux re-injury, (2) Hydrocarbon / Petroleum / Oil Ingestion: inducing gagging/vomiting risks pulmonary aspiration and severe chemical LIPOID PNEUMONIA, (3) Severe Midface Trauma or Basilar Skull Fracture (e.g., battle sign, raccoon eyes, CSF rhinorrhea): risk of intracranial penetration through a fractured cribriform plate into the brain!",
