    "deepUnderstanding": "Your handwritten simulation sheet (`Source_Files/Nasogastric Intubation .pdf`) captures every single detail graded on the BCC-I OSCE station:\n1. **Anatomical Trajectory & Swallowing Coordination**: Advance the lubricated Levin tube horizontally along the **floor of the nasal cavity** (inferior meatus) — never upward toward the cribriform plate! When the tip touches the posterior pharyngeal wall (triggering the CN IX/X gag reflex), have the patient flex their neck slightly forward ('chin to chest' closes the glottic airway and opens the piriform recesses) and **sip/swallow water** as you advance to the **NEX mark** (**Earlobe $\\rightarrow$ Tip of Nose $\\rightarrow$ Epigastrium/Xiphoid**, typically 50–60 cm in adults).\n2. **Safety Verification Before Instilling Anything**: Immediately check that the patient can **speak normally** without coughing or cyanosis (if the tube is in the trachea through the vocal cords, the patient cannot phonate). Then confirm placement by (a) **aspirating gastric fluid** (testing pH $\\le 5.5$), (b) **insufflating 20–30 mL of air with a catheter-tip syringe while auscultating over the epigastrium**, and (c) obtaining a **radiograph** showing the radiopaque tip below the diaphragm in the left upper quadrant.\n3. **Why Caustics & Hydrocarbons Are Contraindicated**: In **caustic alkali/acid ingestion**, blind NG insertion can perforate the softened, necrotic esophageal wall into the mediastinum. In **hydrocarbon/oil ingestion**, low viscosity and low surface tension cause any gag-induced regurgitation to spread rapidly into the bronchial tree, causing severe necrotizing/lipoid pneumonia.",
