    "dailyNotes": "### Complete Handwritten Simulation Notes (`Source_Files/Nasogastric Intubation .pdf` — Session 14)\n#### 13-Step OSCE Execution Sequence\n1. Introduce yourself and confirm patient identity.\n2. Explain the procedure, risks, benefits, and indications.\n3. Obtain informed consent.\n4. Check equipment (NG tube, water-soluble lubricant, 50/60 mL syringe, stethoscope, cup of water with straw, tape, gloves, emesis basin).\n5. Wash hands and put on gloves.\n6. **Measure tube length**: **Earlobe $\\rightarrow$ Tip of Nose $\\rightarrow$ Epigastrium (Xiphoid process)**.\n7. **Position patient**: Sitting upright at **30–45°**.\n8. Apply water-soluble lubricant to the distal tip of the tube.\n9. Insert gently along the nasal floor into the nasopharynx.\n10. Ask the patient to **swallow water** while advancing the tube to the measured mark.\n11. **Check placement (3 ways)**:\n    - Aspirate gastric content.\n    - Push air with syringe while listening (auscultating) over the stomach/epigastrium.\n    - X-ray confirmation.\n12. Secure the tube with tape to the nose.\n13. **Check if the patient can speak** (confirms tube is not through the vocal cords/trachea).\n\n#### Indications, Contraindications & Color-Coded Sizes\n- **3 Indications**:\n  1. *Gastric aspiration/lavage*: Check for Upper GI bleed, replace with saline, or detect pathogens.\n  2. *Decompression*: Bowel obstruction / ileus.\n  3. *Feeding*: Enteral nutrition & medications.\n- **3 Contraindications**:\n  1. *Esophageal injury from caustic ingestion* (perforation risk).\n  2. *Hydrocarbon / oil ingestion* (aspiration $\\rightarrow$ **lipoid pneumonia**).\n  3. *Basilar skull fracture / severe facial trauma* (cribriform plate penetration).\n- **Tube Color & Size Key**:\n  - **Red = 18 Fr** (Largest diameter)\n  - **Orange = 16 Fr** (Standard adult male)\n  - **Green = 14 Fr** (Standard adult female; smaller sizes for children)",
