    "dailyNotes": "MORNING SESSION 16: APPROACH TO NAUSEA, VOMITING & DYSPHAGIA (CD)\nGroup Assignment: Group A (Room 203/1, Facilitator: Dr. Paris Piyaket)\n\n1. PRE-CLASS LECTURE SYNTHESIS (Handouts: Nausea/Vomiting & Dysphagia):\n- Vomiting Mechanism:\n  * Retrograde contractions -> expulsion of gut contents.\n  * Contraction of inspiratory thoracic (diaphragm) and abdominal wall muscles -> high intrathoracic & intra-abdominal pressure -> evacuates stomach.\n  * Gastric cardia herniates above the diaphragm, UES relaxes, glottis closes, soft palate elevates, and larynx moves upward to propel vomitus safely.\n- Emetic Pathways & Antiemetic Targets:\n  * GI Tract / Vagal Afferents (Pharynx, Stomach, Small Intestine): Mechanoreceptors (distension) & chemoreceptors (toxins, chemo) release 5-HT from enterochromaffin (EC) cells -> 5-HT3 receptors -> blocked by Ondansetron.\n  * Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ / Area Postrema, floor of 4th ventricle, outside BBB): D2, 5-HT3, NK1, M1, H1, CB1 receptors -> blocked by Metoclopramide/Domperidone (D2), Ondansetron (5-HT3), Aprepitant (NK1), Dronabinol (CB1).\n  * Vestibular System (Inner Ear / Motion Sickness): H1 & M1 receptors -> blocked by Dimenhydrinate, Promethazine, Scopolamine.\n  * Vomiting Center (Nucleus Tractus Solitarius / Medulla): M1, H1, D2, 5-HT3, NK1.\n\n2. COMPLETE ANSWERS TO THE 3 IN-CLASS CASE SCENARIOS:\n\n=== CASE 1: 30yo Male with 2-Year Dysphagia to Solids & Liquids, Regurgitation, Halitosis, 10-kg Weight Loss ===\n- EGD: Dilated & tortuous esophagus, tight esophagogastric junction (EGJ).\n- Barium Swallow: Dilated lower esophagus with air-barium level (\"bird-beak\" appearance).\n- Q1: How can food regurgitation be differentiated from vomiting?\n  * Regurgitation: Effortless return of undigested, bland (non-acidic, non-bilious) esophageal/gastric contents into the mouth WITHOUT nausea, retching, or forceful abdominal/diaphragmatic muscle contractions; often triggered by supine posture or exercise.\n  * Vomiting: Forceful expulsion of gastroduodenal contents preceded by autonomic prodrome (nausea, hypersalivation, diaphoresis, tachycardia) and accompanied by retching (coordinated contraction of diaphragm, inspiratory intercostals, and abdominal wall muscles).\n- Q2: What are the clinical differences between mechanical obstruction and esophageal motility disorder?\n  * Mechanical obstruction (e.g., stricture, esophageal cancer, Schatzki ring, EoE): Dysphagia starts with SOLID food only (and only progresses to liquids later if lumen narrows severely).\n  * Esophageal motility / neuromuscular disorder (e.g., Achalasia, Diffuse esophageal spasm, Scleroderma): Dysphagia to BOTH solids and liquids right from the onset.\n- Q3: What is the most likely diagnosis and pathophysiology of the disease?\n  * Diagnosis: Primary Achalasia. (Confirm with High-Resolution Esophageal Manometry [HREM] showing impaired LES relaxation [elevated IRP] + absent peristalsis; EGD rules out pseudoachalasia from GEJ cancer).\n  * Pathophysiology: Progressive degeneration/loss of inhibitory myenteric (Auerbach) plexus ganglion cells that release Nitric Oxide (NO) and VIP in the distal esophagus and Lower Esophageal Sphincter (LES). Unopposed excitatory cholinergic (ACh/Substance P) signaling causes failure of LES relaxation during swallowing and aperistalsis of the smooth muscle esophageal body.\n  * Treatment Options: Pneumatic balloon dilation, Laparoscopic Heller myotomy (with partial fundoplication), Peroral Endoscopic Myotomy (POEM), or Botulinum toxin injection / Nitrates / CCBs (nifedipine) if poor surgical candidate.\n\n=== CASE 2: 25yo Woman with Acute Vomiting & Loose Stools 6 Hours After Eating Leftover Fried Rice ===\n- PE: BT 37°C, BP 100/60, HR 120/min, dry lips/tongue, sunken eyeballs, hyperactive bowel sounds, no abdominal tenderness.\n- Q1: What is the most likely diagnosis and pathophysiology of vomiting?\n  * Diagnosis: Acute Food Poisoning (Preformed Enterotoxin-Induced Acute Gastroenteritis) with moderate dehydration/volume depletion.\n  * Pathophysiology of Vomiting: Ingested heat-stable emetic toxin (cereulide) irritates the upper GI mucosa and stimulates enterochromaffin (EC) cells to release serotonin (5-HT), activating vagal afferent 5-HT3 receptors that project to the Nucleus Tractus Solitarius (NTS / Vomiting Center) and Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ).\n- Q2: What is the most likely etiology of her symptoms?\n  * Etiology: Bacillus cereus (emetic toxin = cereulide, classically associated with reheated/leftover fried rice; short incubation period of 1–6 hours). (Alternative short-incubation toxin cause: Staphylococcus aureus enterotoxin).\n- Q3: What medication can be given to relieve her symptoms and what are their mechanisms?\n  * Fluid & Electrolyte Replacement (Cornerstone): Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS; uses Na+-glucose cotransport via SGLT1) or IV isotonic crystalloids (0.9% NaCl / Ringer Lactate) given her tachycardia (HR 120) and dehydration.\n  * Antiemetics:\n    - Ondansetron (5-HT3 receptor antagonist): Blocks peripheral vagal afferent and central CTZ 5-HT3 receptors.\n    - Domperidone or Metoclopramide (D2 receptor antagonists): Block D2 receptors at the CTZ and enhance upper GI prokinetic tone (note: Metoclopramide can cross BBB causing EPS; avoid prokinetics if severe cramping/obstruction).\n  * Note: Antibiotics are NOT indicated because this is a self-limiting preformed toxin illness.\n\n=== CASE 3: 70yo Female with 1-Week Intermittent Bilious Vomiting, Crampy Pain, Distension, Obstipation, and Known 2-cm Gallstone ===\n- PE: BT 36.7°C, BP 100/50, HR 110, RR 22, dry mucous membranes, anicteric sclera; moderately distended, tympanic abdomen with high-pitched (\"tinkling\") bowel sounds, soft, non-tender. No prior surgery.\n- Q1: What is the most likely pathophysiology of vomiting?\n  * Mechanical Intestinal Obstruction: Luminal blockage prevents aboral passage of chyme and secretions -> proximal bowel distension with gas and fluid -> activates intestinal mechanoreceptors (stretch receptors) -> visceral afferent signals via vagal and splanchnic nerves stimulate the medullary Vomiting Center -> retrograde peristalsis and forceful emesis.\n- Q2: Where does the abnormality occur?\n  * Small Intestine DISTAL to the Ampulla of Vater (2nd part of the duodenum), most classically at the Terminal Ileum / Ileocecal Valve (the narrowest part of the small bowel), because the vomitus is BILIOUS (contains bile entering at D2) and there is prominent abdominal distension, high-pitched bowel sounds, and obstipation (inability to pass stool or gas).\n- Q3: What is the differential diagnosis in this patient?\n  * 1. Gallstone Ileus (Top suspect!): Given her age (70yo), lack of prior abdominal surgery (making adhesions unlikely), and known 2-cm gallstone -> chronic pericholecystic inflammation creates a cholecystoenteric (cholecystoduodenal) fistula, allowing the large (>2 cm) stone to pass into the gut and impact at the ileocecal valve. Look for Rigler's Triad on abdominal X-ray/CT: (1) Pneumobilia (air in biliary tree), (2) Small bowel obstruction (dilated loops with air-fluid levels), and (3) Ectopic radiopaque gallstone.\n  * 2. Malignant Bowel Obstruction: Colorectal adenocarcinoma (e.g., cecal/ascending colon mass with incompetent ileocecal valve) or small bowel neoplasm.\n  * 3. Incarcerated Internal or External Hernia (e.g., femoral, inguinal, or obturator hernia in an elderly female).\n  * 4. Volvulus or Stricture (e.g., ischemic or NSAID/Crohn's stricture).",
