    "mcqs": [
      {
        "id": "s23_mcq1",
        "order": "2nd-Order",
        "stem": "A 24-year-old man presents with a 5-month history of crampy right lower quadrant abdominal pain, non-bloody diarrhea, low-grade fever, and a perianal draining fistula. Ileocolonoscopy demonstrates linear serpiginous ulcers interspersed with edematous normal mucosa creating a 'cobblestone' appearance in the terminal ileum and ascending colon, with complete sparing of the rectum. Biopsy shows transmural lymphoid aggregates and non-caseating granulomas. Which pathological complication is most characteristic of this disease compared to its primary inflammatory bowel mimic?",
        "options": [
          "Fibrotic bowel strictures and enteroenteric or perianal fistulae",
          "Primary sclerosing cholangitis with onion-skin bile duct fibrosis",
          "Continuous superficial mucosal ulceration beginning at the anal verge",
          "Inflammatory pseudopolyps confined strictly to the mucosa and submucosa",
          "Fulminant toxic megacolon resulting from selective crypt abscess rupture"
        ],
        "answerIndex": 0,
        "explanation": "This patient has Crohn disease, distinguished by skip lesions, terminal ileal involvement, rectal sparing, cobblestoning, and transmural inflammation with non-caseating granulomas. Because inflammation extends through all layers of the bowel wall to the serosa, Crohn disease causes deep fissures that progress to sinus tracts, enteroenteric/enterovesical/perianal fistulae, and fibrotic strictures ('string sign'). Ulcerative colitis is restricted to the mucosa and submucosa, starting continuously from the rectum."
      },
      {
        "id": "s23_mcq2",
        "order": "2nd-Order",
        "stem": "A 58-year-old man undergoes screening colonoscopy and is found to have a 1.8-cm pedunculated polyp in the sigmoid colon and a 2.2-cm sessile polyp in the rectum. Histological examination of the rectal lesion shows long, finger-like mucosal projections (>75% villous architecture) lined by crowded, hyperchromatic, pseudostratified dysplastic epithelium. In the classic chromosomal instability pathway of colorectal carcinogenesis, which genetic alteration typically initiates the transition from normal colonic epithelium to an early adenomatous polyp?",
        "options": [
          "Activating V600E mutation in the BRAF serine/threonine kinase gene",
          "Homozygous deletion of the TP53 tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 17p",
          "Epigenetic CpG island hypermethylation of the MLH1 mismatch repair promoter",
          "Activating point mutation in the KRAS proto-oncogene activating MAPK signaling",
          "Biallelic inactivation of the APC tumor suppressor gene activating Wnt/beta-catenin"
        ],
        "answerIndex": 4,
        "explanation": "In the Fearon-Vogelstein adenoma-carcinoma sequence (chromosomal instability pathway, accounting for ~80% of sporadic colorectal cancers), the earliest 'first hit/second hit' event is loss of the APC tumor suppressor gene (5q21), which prevents degradation of β-catenin and drives proliferative adenoma formation. Activating KRAS mutations occur next to promote growth into larger/villous adenomas, and loss of TP53 and SMAD4/DCC drives final invasion into carcinoma."
      },
      {
        "id": "s23_mcq3",
        "order": "2nd-Order",
        "stem": "A 71-year-old man presents with a 3-month history of fatigue, dyspnea on exertion, and pale conjunctivae, without any change in stool caliber or visible hematochezia. Laboratory testing shows Hemoglobin 8.2 g/dL (13.5–17.5), MCV 71 fL (80–100), and ferritin 6 ng/mL (30–300). Colonoscopy reveals a large, friable, exophytic mass in the cecum. Why do malignancies in this location typically present with iron deficiency anemia rather than acute bowel obstruction?",
        "options": [
          "Cecal tumors secrete hepcidin-like peptides that block duodenal ferroportin",
          "Right colic arterial collaterals prevent tumor necrosis and visible bleeding",
          "Terminal ileal bile acids lyse red blood cells before stool reaches the rectum",
          "Right-sided tumors invade the circumferentially narrow muscularis propria early",
          "Wide cecal caliber and liquid fecal stream allow exophytic growth without obstruction"
        ],
        "answerIndex": 4,
        "explanation": "Right-sided (cecal and ascending) colon carcinomas grow as bulky, exophytic, polypoid masses into a wide-caliber lumen filled with liquid chyme, so they rarely obstruct and instead cause chronic, insidious occult blood loss and microcytic iron deficiency anemia. Left-sided (descending and sigmoid) carcinomas grow as annular, infiltrating 'napkin-ring' lesions in a narrow lumen with formed stool, presenting with altered bowel habits, pencil-thin stools, and colicky obstruction."
      }
    ]
