        "stem": "A 40-year-old woman presents with 4 months of watery diarrhea (5 stools/day) and right-sided facial flushing with wheezing. Twenty-four-hour urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA) is markedly elevated at 68 mg/24h (normal: 2–8 mg/24h). Contrast CT reveals a 2.8-cm hypervascular mass in the distal ileum AND multiple metastatic nodules in both lobes of the liver. Why did her systemic flushing and wheezing develop only AFTER the tumor metastasized to the liver?",
        "options": [
          "Portal vein hypertension is required to open pulmonary arteriovenous shunts",
          "Primary ileal neuroendocrine tumors synthesize only inactive tryptophan precursors",
          "Primary ileal tumors secrete somatostatin that neutralizes serotonin in the portal vein",
          "Hepatic stellate cells are required to cleave pro-serotonin into active 5-hydroxytryptamine",
          "Bioactive serotonin from a primary midgut tumor is normally inactivated via first-pass hepatic monoamine oxidase"
        ],
        "answerIndex": 4,
        "explanation": "This patient has carcinoid syndrome secondary to a metastatic ileal neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor. When a carcinoid tumor is confined to the gastrointestinal tract (portal drainage), secreted serotonin (5-HT), kallikrein, and histamine travel via the portal vein to the liver and undergo extensive first-pass metabolism by hepatic monoamine oxidase (MAO) into inactive 5-HIAA; once hepatic metastases occur, bioactive mediators are released directly into the hepatic veins and inferior vena cava, reaching the systemic circulation and right heart."
